Codified :: A codified constitution is contained in a single written document that is entrenched and superior to all other law

Uncodified :: An uncodified constitution comes from a variety of different sources and does not represent a higher law

Entrenched :: ‘Entrenched’ means impossible to alter without complicated procedures requiring the agreement of more than just the legislature

Unentrenched :: Unentrenched constitutions have no special procedure for amendment.

Unitary :: A Unitary system is one where all legal sovereignty is contained in a single place.

Federal :: A Federal system is one where legal sovereignty is spread out.

Parliamentary Sovereignty :: The principle that Parliament can make, amend or unmake any law, and cannot bind its successors or be bound by its predecessors. It has sovereignty

Rule of Law :: The principle that all people and bodies, including government, must follow the law and can be held to account if they do not.

Statute Law :: Laws passed by Parliament.

Common Law :: Laws made by judges where the law does not cover the issue or is unclear

Convention :: Traditions not contained in law but influential in the operation of a political system. e.g. salisbury-convention

Authoratitive Works :: Works written by experts describing how a political system is run, they are not legally binding but are taken as significant guides.

Treaties :: Formal agreements with other countries, usually ratified by Parliament.


